                                                      "thinking": "I'll check the `docs` directory for any \"What's New\" or \"New Features\" files.\nActually, the user might be asking about the features of \"pi\" (the coding agent).\nI'll summarize the main features from the README and the docs.\n\nKey features to highlight:\n1. **Interactive Mode**: Editor, commands, keyboard shortcuts, and a message queue (steering/follow-up).\n2. **Sessions & Branching**: JSONL session files with in-place branching (`/tree`) and cloning.\n3. **Context Management**: Compaction (summarizing old messages) and context files (`AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md`).\n4. **Extensibility**:\n    - **Prompt Templates**: Reusable prompts.\n    - **Skills**: On-demand capability packages (Agent Skills standard).\n    - **Extensions**: TypeScript modules for tools, commands, UI components, and event handlers.\n    - **Themes**: Hot-reloading themes.\n    - **Pi Packages**: Bundling and sharing resources via npm/git.\n5. **Programmatic Usage**: SDK for embedding in apps and RPC mode for process integration.\n6. **Tooling**: Built-in `read`, `write`, `edit`, `bash`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`.\n7. **Support for multiple providers**: Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, DeepSeek, NVIDIA NIM, etc., plus `llama.cpp` integration.\n8. **Project Trust**: Security-conscious loading of project-local resources.\n\nI should also check `docs/compaction.md` and `docs/extensions.md` to see if there are any \"latest\" highlights there.\nActually, the prompt is \"Explain the latest pi features\".\nI'll check if there's a \"latest\" section or if I should just explain the main ones.\n\nI'll check `docs/compaction.md` specifically as it's often a \"new\" feature in these agents.",
                                                      "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"
                                                    },
                                                    {
                                                      "type": "text",
                                                      "text": "Pi is a minimal, highly extensible terminal coding agent designed to be adapted to your specific workflow. Its \"latest\" features and core capabilities center around flexibility, session management, and deep customization.\n\nHere are the key features of the current version of pi:\n\n### 1. Interactive & Session Management\n*   **Session Branching & Tree View**: Instead of creating new files for every turn, pi uses a JSONL-based session format that allows for in-place branching. You can use `/tree` to navigate different branches of a conversation, jump to any point in the history, and switch between them seamlessly.\n*   **Context Compaction**: To handle long conversations without exhausting the model's context window, pi automatically (or manually via `/compact`) summarizes older messages while keeping recent ones intact.\n*   **Session Cloning & Forking**: You can easily clone the current active branch into a new session file (`/clone`) or fork a specific previous point in a session (`/fork`) to start a new task.\n\n### 2. Advanced Extensibility (The \"Pi Package\" Ecosystem)\nPi is designed to be extended rather than modified. You can bundle and share resources using **Pi Packages**:\n*   **Extensions**: Written in TypeScript, these can add custom tools, commands, keyboard shortcuts, event handlers, and even custom UI components (like widgets or custom editors).\n*   **Skills**: Follows the Agent Skills standard to provide on-demand capabilities that the model can call when needed.\n*   **Prompt Templates**: Reusable prompt snippets that can be expanded in the editor using `/template`.\n*   **Themes**: Support for hot-reloading themes, allowing you to customize the look and feel of the terminal interface instantly.\n\n### 3. Workflow & UI Features\n*   **Message Queue**: While the agent is working, you can queue \"steering\" messages (delivered after the current turn) or \"follow-up\" messages (delivered after all work is finished) using `Enter` and `Alt+Enter`.\n*   **Project Trust**: A security feature that requires you to explicitly trust a project folder before pi loads project-local settings, resources, or extensions.\n*   **Context Files**: Automatically loads `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` files from your project to provide the model with project-specific instructions and conventions.\n*   **Multi-Model & Provider Support**: Native support for a vast range of providers (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, DeepSeek, NVIDIA NIM, etc.) and local inference via `llama.cpp`.\n\n### 4. Programmatic Integration\n*   **SDK**: A TypeScript SDK allows you to embed pi's agentic capabilities into your own applications.\n*   **RPC Mode**: Provides a way to integrate pi into other processes via a standardized JSONL-framed RPC protocol.\n\n### 5. Built-in Tooling\nBy default, pi provides the model with a robust set of tools to interact with your filesystem:\n*   `read`, `write`, `edit`\n*   `bash` (with session environment variables like `PI_SESSION_ID`)\n*   `grep`, `find`, `ls`"
                                                    }
