                                                                                              "type": "text",
                                                                                              "text": "To parse a pi session using the Pi SDK, you can use the `SessionManager` to open the session file and then interact with the session object to retrieve entries, the tree structure, or the context.\n\nHere is the TypeScript code to parse and print the details of the specified session:\n\n```typescript\nimport { SessionManager } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n\nasync function parseSession(sessionPath: string) {\n  try {\n    // Open the session file using the SessionManager\n    // SessionManager.open returns a session object that holds the state\n    const { session } = await createAgentSession({\n      sessionManager: SessionManager.open(sessionPath),\n    });\n\n    console.log(`--- Session Metadata ---`);\n    console.log(`ID: ${session.sessionId}`);\n    console.log(`CWD: ${session.cwd}`);\n    console.log(`File: ${session.sessionFile}`);\n    console.log(`Name: ${session.getSessionName()}`);\n\n    console.log(`\\n--- Session Tree ---`);\n    // Get the full tree structure of the session\n    const tree = session.getTree();\n    console.log(JSON.stringify(tree, null, 2));\n\n    console.log(`\\n--- Active Entries (Context) ---`);\n    // buildContextEntries() returns the entries on the path from leaf to root,\n    // handling compaction automatically.\n    const contextEntries = session.buildContextEntries();\n    for (const entry of contextEntries) {\n      console.log(`[${entry.timestamp}] Type: ${entry.type} | ID: ${entry.id}`);\n      \n      // If it's a message, print some content\n      if (entry.type === \"message\" && entry.message) {\n        const role = entry.message.role;\n        const content = JSON.stringify(entry.message.content);\n        console.log(`  -> ${role}: ${content}`);\n      } else if (entry.type === \"compaction\") {\n        console.log(`  -> Compaction: ${entry.summary}`);\n      } else if (entry.type === \"model_change\") {\n        console.log(`  -> Model Changed: ${entry.provider}/${entry.modelId}`);\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Cleanup\n    session.dispose();\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error(\"Error parsing session:\", error);\n  }\n}\n\n// Helper function to match the SDK's createAgentSession pattern if needed,\n// but SessionManager.open is the direct way to get a session handle.\nasync function createAgentSession(options: any) {\n  const { createAgentSession: piCreateSession } = await import(\"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\");\n  return piCreateSession(options);\n}\n\nconst path = \"/root/.pi/agent/sessions/--home-pi-random-2026-07-31--/2026-07-31T18-11-28-299Z_019fb95f-c1ab-7dc7-b157-268a9a1226bf.jsonl\";\nparseSession(path);\n```\n\n### Key SDK methods used:\n1.  **`SessionManager.open(path)`**: Loads a specific `.jsonl` session file into a manageable object.\n2.  **`session.getTree()`**: Returns the complete hierarchical structure of the session (all branches).\n3.  **`session.buildContextEntries()`**: This is the most important method for \"parsing\" the active conversation. It walks from the current leaf back to the root, automatically handling **Compaction** logic (where older parts of the history are replaced by summaries).\n4.  **`session.getSessionName()`**: Retrieves the display name set via `/name` or `--name`.\n5.  **`session.dispose()`**: Properly closes the session handle."
